  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="cost-title">One purchase, many sales</h2>
      <p>Decide how a vehicle's cost spreads across what it yields, then use the same method every time. This is an illustration with invented figures, not a required method. Ask an accountant what suits your business.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Illustration of spreading a vehicle's cost, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">What came out</th><th scope="col">Expected sale value</th><th scope="col">Share of cost if bought for ₹60,000</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Engine and gearbox</th><td>₹40,000</td><td>₹24,000</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Body panels and glass</th><td>₹25,000</td><td>₹15,000</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Tyres and battery</th><td>₹15,000</td><td>₹9,000</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Metal sold as scrap</th><td>₹20,000</td><td>₹12,000</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Total</th><td>₹1,00,000</td><td>₹60,000</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="para">Unsold parts remain stock until they sell. A yard full of old panels may be worth far less than the cost allotted to them, so review them regularly.</p>
