<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="buy-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="buy-title">Buying from vehicle owners and small sellers</h2>
      <p>Sellers are often individuals with no GST invoice. Keep your own record anyway: date, seller's name and contact, vehicle number, price and how it was paid. A copy of the sale paperwork helps show where the vehicle came from.</p>
      <p class="para">Rule 138 of the CGST Rules covers movement of goods worth more than ₹50,000 by a registered person, including inward supply from an unregistered person. Ask how it applies when you bring vehicles or scrap in, and whether tax is payable on any purchase from an unregistered seller.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Selling used parts</h2>
      <p>Rule 32(5) of the CGST Rules covers a person dealing in second-hand goods, used as such or after minor processing that does not change their nature. Where no input tax credit was taken on the purchase, the value of supply is the difference between selling and purchase prices. A negative difference is ignored.</p>
      <p class="para">Ask whether parts cut from a vehicle fit this rule, as it was not written with dismantling in mind. The cost method above matters here, because the margin depends on it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="scrap-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="scrap-title">Selling scrap metal</h2>
      <p>Section 394(1) of the Income-tax Act, 2025 lists sale of scrap among the receipts on which a seller collects tax. The Act as enacted shows 1%. A Railway Board letter of June 2026 applies 2%, citing the Finance Act, 2026. Check the current rate, the section's definition of scrap, and any exception where the buyer gives a declaration.</p>
      <p class="para">Scrap is usually sold by weight, so keep weighbridge slips with the bill. Mixed loads priced as one lot make margin hard to check.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>If you scrap vehicles officially</h2>
      <p>The Core reported in February 2026 that a few licensed shops here handle paperwork and issue Certificates of Destruction to vehicle owners. If you do, keep each certificate with the purchase and disposal records for that vehicle, so that one file answers any later question.</p>
      <p class="para">Vehicle scrapping and pollution rules are outside this page. Take specific advice on them, and keep licence and compliance costs in their own account.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="ask-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="ask-title">Moving from cash to clean records</h2>
      <p>The same report describes much of this trade as informal and cash-based. If you are starting to organise your books, you do not need to fix everything at once. Begin with these:</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>A separate bank account for the business</li>
        <li>A numbered register of every vehicle bought</li>
        <li>A daily note of sales and collections</li>
        <li>A question to your accountant about old years, before you file anything new</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>About the area, and about us</h2>
      <p>Delhi's Department of Industries lists Mayapuri Industrial Area, Phases I and II. Press reports trace the scrap market to the 1970s, and describe spare part shops alongside heavy dismantling units. This page describes a trade, and does not suggest anything about any particular business.</p>
      <p class="para">AccountOrbit has no office in Mayapuri and does not publish who is available here. Being named on this page does not mean a professional is based nearby.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

